{
  "id": "greek",
  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "at-a-glance", "topic": "overview", "title": "Greek at a glance",
      "body": [
        "Modern Greek is the official language of Greece and Cyprus, spoken by about 13 million people. It is its own branch of the Indo-European family, and it has been written in the Greek alphabet for nearly three thousand years.",
        "Nouns have three genders and change their ending for case; the article in front shows both. Verbs mark the person with an ending, so pronouns are usually left out. Word order is flexible, though subject–verb–object is the most common."
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "t": "{Μιλάω} λίγα ελληνικά.", "r": "Miláo líga elliniká.", "e": "I speak a little Greek." }
      ],
      "tags": ["Indo-European", "gender", "cases"]
    },
    {
      "id": "alphabet", "topic": "script", "title": "The alphabet",
      "body": [
        "Greek has 24 letters, each with a capital and a small form. Sigma has two small forms: σ inside a word and ς at the end.",
        "Several letters look like Latin ones but sound different: Η η is i, Ρ ρ is r, Ν ν is n, Υ υ is i, Χ χ is the ch in Bach, and Β β is v."
      ],
      "table": {
        "head": ["Letter", "Name", "Sound"],
        "rows": [
          ["Α α", "άλφα", "a"], ["Β β", "βήτα", "v"], ["Γ γ", "γάμμα", "gh, or y before e/i"],
          ["Δ δ", "δέλτα", "th as in this"], ["Ε ε", "έψιλον", "e"], ["Ζ ζ", "ζήτα", "z"],
          ["Η η", "ήτα", "i"], ["Θ θ", "θήτα", "th as in thin"], ["Ι ι", "γιώτα", "i"],
          ["Κ κ", "κάππα", "k"], ["Λ λ", "λάμδα", "l"], ["Μ μ", "μι", "m"],
          ["Ν ν", "νι", "n"], ["Ξ ξ", "ξι", "ks"], ["Ο ο", "όμικρον", "o"],
          ["Π π", "πι", "p"], ["Ρ ρ", "ρο", "r (tapped)"], ["Σ σ ς", "σίγμα", "s"],
          ["Τ τ", "ταυ", "t"], ["Υ υ", "ύψιλον", "i"], ["Φ φ", "φι", "f"],
          ["Χ χ", "χι", "ch as in Bach"], ["Ψ ψ", "ψι", "ps"], ["Ω ω", "ωμέγα", "o"]
        ]
      },
      "tags": ["script", "letters"]
    },
    {
      "id": "digraphs", "topic": "sounds", "title": "Letter pairs",
      "body": "Some pairs of letters make one sound. Several spellings share the sound i, so spelling has to be learned word by word.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Pair", "Sound", "Example"],
        "rows": [
          ["ει, οι, υι", "i", "{ει}μαι, “I am”"],
          ["αι", "e", "κ{αι}, “and”"],
          ["ου", "u", "π{ου}, “where”"],
          ["αυ, ευ", "av / ev, or af / ef", "{αυ}τός, “he”"],
          ["μπ", "b (mb inside a word)", "{μπ}ύρα, “beer”"],
          ["ντ", "d (nd inside a word)", "{ντ}ομάτα, “tomato”"],
          ["γκ, γγ", "g (ng inside a word)", "{γκ}αράζ, “garage”"],
          ["τσ, τζ", "ts, dz", "{τσ}άι, “tea”"]
        ]
      },
      "tags": ["pronunciation", "digraphs"]
    },
    {
      "id": "stress", "topic": "sounds", "title": "The stress mark",
      "body": "Every word of more than one syllable carries an accent (τόνος) on its stressed vowel. It matters: πότε means “when?” but ποτέ means “never”. Capitals written in full (ΑΘΗΝΑ) drop the accent.",
      "examples": [
        { "t": "π{ό}τε · ποτ{έ}", "r": "póte · poté", "e": "when? · never" },
        { "t": "ν{ό}μος · νομ{ό}ς", "r": "nómos · nomós", "e": "law · prefecture" }
      ],
      "tags": ["stress", "accent"]
    },
    {
      "id": "punctuation", "topic": "script", "title": "Punctuation",
      "body": "Greek writes the question mark as ; and the raised dot · as a semicolon. Commas and full stops are the same as in English.",
      "examples": [
        { "t": "Τι κάνεις{;}", "r": "Ti kánis?", "e": "How are you?" }
      ],
      "tags": ["punctuation"]
    },
    {
      "id": "articles", "topic": "grammar", "title": "The article",
      "body": "“The” agrees with its noun in gender, number and case. The forms below are the ones a beginner meets most: subject (nominative) and object (accusative).",
      "table": {
        "head": ["", "Masculine", "Feminine", "Neuter"],
        "rows": [
          ["Subject, singular", "ο", "η", "το"],
          ["Object, singular", "τον", "την", "το"],
          ["Subject, plural", "οι", "οι", "τα"],
          ["Object, plural", "τους", "τις", "τα"],
          ["“a, one”", "ένας", "μία / μια", "ένα"]
        ]
      },
      "tags": ["articles", "gender", "cases"]
    },
    {
      "id": "noun-endings", "topic": "grammar", "title": "Common noun endings",
      "body": "The ending of a noun usually gives away its gender, and tells you how it makes its plural.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["Gender", "Singular", "Plural", "Example"],
        "rows": [
          ["Masculine", "-ος", "-οι", "ο φίλος → οι φίλοι"],
          ["Masculine", "-ας", "-ες", "ο άντρας → οι άντρες"],
          ["Masculine", "-ης", "-ες", "ο μαθητής → οι μαθητές"],
          ["Feminine", "-α", "-ες", "η ώρα → οι ώρες"],
          ["Feminine", "-η", "-ες", "η φωνή → οι φωνές"],
          ["Neuter", "-ο", "-α", "το βιβλίο → τα βιβλία"],
          ["Neuter", "-ι", "-ια", "το σπίτι → τα σπίτια"],
          ["Neuter", "-μα", "-ματα", "το όνομα → τα ονόματα"]
        ]
      },
      "tags": ["nouns", "plural"]
    },
    {
      "id": "present", "topic": "verbs", "title": "The present tense",
      "body": "Two main patterns. Verbs stressed before the ending (group A) and verbs stressed on the ending (group B). Είμαι, “to be”, is irregular.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["", "γράφω, write", "μιλάω, speak", "είμαι, be"],
        "rows": [
          ["I", "γράφω", "μιλάω", "είμαι"],
          ["you", "γράφεις", "μιλάς", "είσαι"],
          ["he, she, it", "γράφει", "μιλάει", "είναι"],
          ["we", "γράφουμε", "μιλάμε", "είμαστε"],
          ["you (pl.)", "γράφετε", "μιλάτε", "είστε"],
          ["they", "γράφουν", "μιλάνε", "είναι"]
        ]
      },
      "tags": ["verbs", "present"]
    },
    {
      "id": "numbers", "topic": "numbers", "title": "Numbers",
      "body": "One, three and four change with the gender of the noun (ένας / μία / ένα, τρεις / τρία, τέσσερις / τέσσερα). The rest do not.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["", "Greek", "Romanization"],
        "rows": [
          ["1", "ένα", "éna"], ["2", "δύο", "dío"], ["3", "τρία", "tría"], ["4", "τέσσερα", "téssera"],
          ["5", "πέντε", "pénde"], ["6", "έξι", "éxi"], ["7", "επτά / εφτά", "eptá / eftá"],
          ["8", "οκτώ / οχτώ", "októ / ochtó"], ["9", "εννέα / εννιά", "ennéa / enniá"], ["10", "δέκα", "déka"],
          ["20", "είκοσι", "íkosi"], ["100", "εκατό", "ekató"], ["1000", "χίλια", "chília"]
        ]
      },
      "tags": ["numbers"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrases", "topic": "phrases", "title": "Everyday phrases",
      "examples": [
        { "t": "Γεια σας.", "r": "Giá sas.", "e": "Hello. (polite or to several people)" },
        { "t": "Καλημέρα.", "r": "Kaliméra.", "e": "Good morning." },
        { "t": "Ευχαριστώ πολύ.", "r": "Efcharistó polí.", "e": "Thank you very much." },
        { "t": "Παρακαλώ.", "r": "Parakaló.", "e": "Please. / You're welcome." },
        { "t": "Συγγνώμη, δεν καταλαβαίνω.", "r": "Signómi, den katalavéno.", "e": "Sorry, I don't understand." },
        { "t": "Πόσο κάνει;", "r": "Póso káni?", "e": "How much is it?" },
        { "t": "Τον λογαριασμό, παρακαλώ.", "r": "Ton logariasmó, parakaló.", "e": "The bill, please." },
        { "t": "Στην υγειά μας!", "r": "Stin igiá mas!", "e": "Cheers! (to our health)" }
      ],
      "tags": ["phrases", "travel"]
    },
    {
      "id": "nai-ochi", "topic": "culture", "title": "Ναι means yes",
      "body": "Ναι (ne) is “yes” and όχι (óchi) is “no”, the reverse of what English ears expect. In conversation a small upward nod with raised eyebrows, often with a click of the tongue, also means no.",
      "tags": ["culture", "gestures"]
    }
  ]
}
